ss both peasants have nothing safe to play. Since we *can* beat it, and our teammate may be holding low cards or bombs, it\'s better for us to play.\n\nWe should choose the **lowest card that wins**, to conserve high cards for future critical moments.\n\nLowest winning single in our hand: **♥8** (8 is the smallest rank above 6).\n\nWhy not pass?  \nIf we pass, and teammate cannot beat 6 (possible, since they might have only high or very low cards), landlord keeps control. But teammate passed last time after a strong play — hard to say.\n\nHowever, teammate played a long sequence earlier (♥9 ♥10 ♥J ♦Q ♣K ♠A), suggesting they had mid-high cards, possibly now depleted. They may not have a card >6.\n\nSo we should act.\n\nBest move: **Play ♥8** — just barely beats ♠6, preserves our higher cards (like 2s and Aces) for when they’re truly needed.\n\nReason:  \nTo minimize waste of high-value cards while maintaining pressure on the landlord and avoiding giving them free turns. Playing the minimal winning card (♥8) achieves this. Our teammate is close to emptying their hand, so keeping control in peasant hands is crucial.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥8"]}'}
